Findings  ✗ 6 errors · ⚠ 10 warnings · 4 info

   1  ✗ LOG_LEVEL in .env has the invalid value verbose: expected one of debug,
        info, warn or error
        error · EG014 invalid-value · .env:2:11

      Why      The app expects LOG_LEVEL to be one of debug, info, warn or
               error; an invalid value usually crashes it at startup or, worse,
               is silently misread.
      Fix      1. Set LOG_LEVEL to one of debug, info, warn or error in .env:2.
               2. If the schema is wrong, update LOG_LEVEL at .envguard.yaml:7.
      Related  .envguard.yaml:7:3

   2  ✗ DATABASE_URL in .env.example looks like a URL with an embedded password
        error · EG010 possible-secret-in-template · .env.example:4:14

      Why      A template is shared with everyone who can read the repository; a
               real credential there is leaked, and it stays in git history even
               after it is removed.
      Fix      1. Replace the value with an empty one or a placeholder
                  (`DATABASE_URL=` or `DATABASE_URL=<your-value>`).
               2. If it is a real credential, rotate it now: it has been
                  exposed.
               3. If it is a deliberate fake, use an obvious placeholder or add
                  `# envguard-ignore EG010` on the line above.
      Evidence detected: URL with an embedded password

   3  ✗ STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is declared in .env.production.example but missing
        from .env.production
        error · EG001 missing-variable · .env.production

      Why      Anything that reads STRIPE_SECRET_KEY in the production
               environment gets no value, which usually fails only at runtime or
               silently falls back to a default.
      Fix      1. Add `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=` to .env.production (it is declared in
                  .env.production.example:2) and set its value.
               2. If it is not needed there, mark it optional in the template
                  (`STRIPE_SECRET_KEY= # optional`) or put
                  `# envguard-ignore EG001` on the line above it in
                  .env.production.example.
      Related  .env.production.example:2:1

   4  ✗ WORKERS in .env.production has the invalid value 0: is below the minimum
        1
        error · EG014 invalid-value · .env.production:6:9

      Why      The app expects WORKERS to be an integer between 1 and 64; an
               invalid value usually crashes it at startup or, worse, is
               silently misread.
      Fix      1. Set WORKERS to an integer between 1 and 64 in
                  .env.production:6.
               2. If the schema is wrong, update WORKERS at .envguard.yaml:15.
      Related  .envguard.yaml:15:3

   5  ✗ missing '=' after key
        error · EG007 syntax-error · .env.production:10:8

      Why      Env loaders skip or misread malformed lines, so the affected
               variable silently ends up with a wrong value or none at all.
      Fix      1. Write assignments as KEY=value.
               2. Keys cannot contain spaces, and a bare 'export KEY' without a
                  value is not supported.

   6  ✗ ANALYTICS_WRITE_KEY is read by the code but not declared in any env
        file, template or schema
        error · EG003 used-but-undeclared · src/server.js:23:33

      Why      Nothing documents that ANALYTICS_WRITE_KEY must be set, so a new
               environment or teammate will not provide it and the code reads an
               empty value at runtime.
      Fix      1. Add `ANALYTICS_WRITE_KEY=` to .env.example (read at
                  src/server.js:23), then set it in each real env file.
               2. If the platform provides it, add ANALYTICS_WRITE_KEY to
                  scanner.ignore_variables in .envguard.yaml.

   7  ⚠ SESSION_SECRET is empty in .env but required by .env.example
        warning · EG005 empty-required-value · .env:5:1

      Why      An empty SESSION_SECRET is usually read as "set" and passes
               presence checks, then fails later (empty URLs, empty credentials,
               zero ports).
      Fix      1. Set a value for SESSION_SECRET in .env:5.
               2. If an empty value is intentional, mark the template line
                  optional: `SESSION_SECRET= # optional` in .env.example:6.
      Related  .env.example:6:1

   8  ⚠ FEATURE_NEW_CHECKOUT is set in .env but not documented in any template
        warning · EG002 undocumented-variable · .env:8:1

      Why      Teammates, CI and deploy targets learn which variables exist from
               the templates; without an entry, FEATURE_NEW_CHECKOUT will be
               missing wherever this file is not copied.
      Fix      Add `FEATURE_NEW_CHECKOUT=` (with an empty or placeholder value,
               never the real one) to .env.example, or remove it from .env if
               nothing uses it.

   9  ⚠ CACHE_DIR is set in .env but not documented in any template
        warning · EG002 undocumented-variable · .env:9:1

      Why      Teammates, CI and deploy targets learn which variables exist from
               the templates; without an entry, CACHE_DIR will be missing
               wherever this file is not copied.
      Fix      Add `CACHE_DIR=` (with an empty or placeholder value, never the
               real one) to .env.example, or remove it from .env if nothing uses
               it.

  10  ⚠ CACHE_DIR references APP_HOME, which is not defined in .env or the env
        files loaded with it
        warning · EG016 unresolved-reference · .env:9:11

      Why      Loaders that expand references substitute an empty string for an
               undefined APP_HOME, so CACHE_DIR silently ends up incomplete (for
               example a URL without a host).
      Fix      Define `APP_HOME=` in .env (checked: .env and .env.example), give
               the reference a default such as `${APP_HOME:-value}`, or add
               APP_HOME to scanner.ignore_variables in .envguard.yaml if the
               process environment provides it.

  11  ⚠ REDIS_URL points to a local address in production (.env.production)
        warning · EG015 unsafe-production-value · .env.production:4:11

      Why      Inside a production container or server, localhost is the machine
               itself, not your database or API; this is almost always a
               development value that slipped through.
      Fix      Set REDIS_URL in .env.production to the production host.

  12  ⚠ DEBUG is defined 2 times in .env.production (lines 7 and 8)
        warning · EG006 duplicate-key · .env.production:8:1

      Why      Most loaders (dotenv, python-dotenv, Docker) silently use the
               last definition, so editing the first one has no effect and the
               file says two different things.
      Fix      Keep one definition of DEBUG in .env.production: delete line 8 or
               the earlier one at line 7, keeping the value you intend.
      Evidence line 7: false; line 8: true
      Related  .env.production:7:1

  13  ⚠ DEBUG is enabled in production (.env.production)
        warning · EG015 unsafe-production-value · .env.production:8:7

      Why      debug mode in production exposes stack traces, settings and
               sometimes an interactive console to anyone who triggers an error.
      Fix      Set `DEBUG=false` in .env.production.

  14  ⚠ STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is declared for production but not for staging
        warning · EG013 environment-drift · .env.production.example:2:1

      Why      Environments that are supposed to run the same code should need
               the same variables; a variable documented for only some of them
               is often forgotten when the next deploy reaches the others.
      Fix      1. Add `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=` to .env.staging.example, or move it
                  to .env.example if every environment needs it.
               2. If it really applies only to production, add
                  `# envguard-ignore EG013` above .env.production.example:2.

  15  ⚠ STAGING_BANNER is declared for staging but not for production
        warning · EG013 environment-drift · .env.staging.example:2:1

      Why      Environments that are supposed to run the same code should need
               the same variables; a variable documented for only some of them
               is often forgotten when the next deploy reaches the others.
      Fix      1. Add `STAGING_BANNER=` to .env.production.example, or move it
                  to .env.example if every environment needs it.
               2. If it really applies only to staging, add
                  `# envguard-ignore EG013` above .env.staging.example:2.

  16  ⚠ docker-compose.yml interpolates ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}, which is not
        defined in .env (the file Compose reads) or any template
        warning · EG011 compose-undefined-variable · docker-compose.yml:17:28

      Why      Compose substitutes an empty string for an unset
               POSTGRES_PASSWORD and only prints a warning, so the container
               starts with a broken value.
      Fix      Add `POSTGRES_PASSWORD=` with a value to .env and document it in
               a template, or give it a default in the Compose file:
               `${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:-value}`.

  17  • LEGACY_API_KEY is declared in .env.example but never read by the code,
        env files, Compose files or workflows
        info · EG004 declared-but-unused · .env.example:9:1

      Why      Unused variables make templates longer and send people looking
               for values nothing needs; they are often left over after a
               refactor. Note: 1 dynamic lookup in this app (a variable name
               computed at run time) could not be analyzed and may read it.
      Fix      1. If nothing needs LEGACY_API_KEY, remove it from .env.example:9
                  and from the real env files.
               2. If a tool outside the scanned code reads it (Prisma, Docker, a
                  framework), add LEGACY_API_KEY to scanner.ignore_variables in
                  .envguard.yaml.

  18  • workflows use secret FLY_API_TOKEN, but no template or schema documents
        it
        info · EG012 ci-secret-undocumented · .github/workflows/deploy.yml:14:30

      Why      Whoever sets up a fork, a new environment or a new repository has
               to discover FLY_API_TOKEN by reading workflow files; a missing
               one usually shows up as a failed deploy.
      Fix      Document it: add `FLY_API_TOKEN=` to .env.example with a comment
               such as
               `# CI: set in GitHub → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions (Secrets tab)`,
               or declare it under `variables:` in .envguard.yaml.

  19  • workflows use variable DEPLOY_REGION, but no template or schema
        documents it
        info · EG012 ci-secret-undocumented · .github/workflows/deploy.yml:15:30

      Why      Whoever sets up a fork, a new environment or a new repository has
               to discover DEPLOY_REGION by reading workflow files; a missing
               one usually shows up as a failed deploy.
      Fix      Document it: add `DEPLOY_REGION=` to .env.example with a comment
               such as
               `# CI: set in GitHub → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions (Variables tab)`,
               or declare it under `variables:` in .envguard.yaml.

  20  • WORKER_CONCURRENCY is read by the code but not declared in any env file,
        template or schema
        info · EG003 used-but-undeclared · worker/main.py:6:30

      Why      The code falls back to a default, so nothing breaks if
               WORKER_CONCURRENCY is unset, but it is invisible to anyone
               configuring the app and the default may be wrong for production.
      Fix      1. Document it by adding `WORKER_CONCURRENCY= # optional` to
                  .env.example (read at worker/main.py:6).
               2. If you do not want it documented, add WORKER_CONCURRENCY to
                  scanner.ignore_variables in .envguard.yaml.

